          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "CBT-I is described as first-line for chronic insomnia in adults because its benefits last after treatment ends, and it avoids the risks of long-term sleeping-pill use. Medication is second-line and is meant to be short-term. The guidance is from sleep medicine and internal medicine bodies (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*).\n\n**Durability:** The guideline says CBT-I's effects persist after treatment ends, whereas drug effects generally do not. The general insomnia reference adds that non-drug treatment \"responds durably,\" so long-term reliance on hypnotics can be avoided for most people (*Insomnia*).\n\n**Adverse effects:** The guideline says CBT-I avoids the harms linked to long-term hypnotic use. It also notes that over-the-counter antihistamine sleep aids have limited evidence and notable anticholinergic side effects, particularly in older adults. The retrieved passages do not list the adverse-effect profiles of prescription hypnotics, so I can't compare them in detail.\n\n**Comparative efficacy:** The retrieved passages do not include head-to-head trial results or effect sizes. I can't tell you how much better CBT-I performs than medication in the short term, and I won't fill that gap from memory. The evidence strength behind the recommendation is also not graded in these passages.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** The insomnia guidance has no disagreement section, and my search found no conflicting insomnia recommendations in the sources. The disagreements the search returned concern urinary infections, sore throats and osteoarthritis, so they don't apply here.\n\n**Practical points:**\n- CBT-I usually runs about four to eight sessions. Its components include stimulus control and sleep restriction. The guidance says sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment.\n- Medication, if used, should involve shared decision-making, the shortest appropriate duration, and a plan for stopping agreed at the start.\n- Some signs call for assessment beyond insomnia treatment, including loud snoring with witnessed pauses in breathing, excessive daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, acting out dreams, or sleep problems alongside low mood and hopelessness (*Insomnia*). If you have any of these, raise them with a clinician.\n\nFor a decision about your own sleep, a clinician can weigh these points against your history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
